exactly what's going to  happen because you can't solve for the  variables even if you knew what they are  and so you need to compensate by doing a  lot of exploratory data analysis  and typically in my experience in a  molecular dynamics project  exploratory data analysis gets published  at about a 10  rate such that you do a lot of  exploratory data analysis  oh this doesn't indicate an underlying  kinetic or thermodynamic mechanism  and so we're going to throw that  analysis out and we're going to proceed  with a different type of analysis  you have to be fast in doing this  otherwise you will drown in the  amount of exploratory data analysis you  need to do  and the wolfram language is uniquely  poised to expedite that workflow  so the different types of  post-processing you'll do are  visualizations simple snapshots as you  see here  quantitative analysis to get at the  kinetic and thermodynamic mechanisms  quantitatively  like you would do in a traditional  physics problem and quantitative  analysis such that you understand  exactly what is going on physically in  the simulation  for example structural inferences etc  the  spatial statistics for example  so visualization is obviously useful you  know mathematic excuse me the wolfram  language is great at that  it uh is incredibly useful to see what's  happening before you go to analyze it  you know a lot of people are visual  learners you know those those kind of  cues are important in  not only understanding what happened in  the simulation but in corroborating  the observations that can be made  experimentally so here are  visualizations associated with the  projects that i worked on for the  nanoparticle self-assembly  where you can see the plot label here  varies from  left to right increasing salt  concentrations so as you titrate salt  you see a pretty drastic variation in  the propensity for self-assembly where  in some ionic strength conditions salt  conditions  you see extensive self-assembly and then