ay taking snapshots and putting them in  a powerpoint is incredibly valuable and  will triple the amount of projects you  can work on per unit time if you do it  properly  and so moving on to quantitative  analysis  how do you enhance this picture  with quantitative data we'll  brush through this quickly such that you  have the snapshots that you have on the  bottom left  you have the same titration and salt on  the x-axis for both quantitative plots  you see here  the top left is an experimental  assessment of the optical density  so how opaque is that solution where it  peaks for assembly conditions and goes  down for non-assembly conditions  in some forms of light scattering the  radius of gyration is what determines  that  we've used a wfr that i've created to  give an estimate of the radius of  gyration of these particles  the uh assemblies rather and you can see  that while it's not a one-to-one  correspondence we wouldn't expect it to  be in this case  there are the same behaviors where you  have a domain of assembly and  domains of non-assembly  this ability to take a time average with  say a wolfram function repository  function  expedite your workflow orders of  magnitude over what you would come to  think c plus plus could do right if you  did all of this coding in c  plus we'll get to a more quantitative  example later  but it will slow you down considerably  one other example of neat quantitative  analysis that you can do  with these kind of systems is on the  left you have a cannonball stacking  it's called an fcc or hcp lattice  i believe hcp here but don't quote me on  that  the two are very similar they're close  packed sphere rigidly spherical lattices  and a cannonball sagging is what you  would see if you walked into a war  memorial you walked in onto a pirate  ship  etc from the visualization in the center  you can kind of think  perhaps that's a similar stacking and if  you look at the radial distribution  function also known as a pair  correla